CAT = "trcnTeaching"; SEG = "TCH"
# Pedagogy: Instructional Planning, Teaching Methodologies, Classroom Management, Differentiation.
Q = [
("A teacher writes the objective \"Pupils will understand fractions.\" What is the main weakness of this objective?",
 "It cannot be observed or measured", ["It names a maths topic", "It is too short to use", "It mentions the pupils"],
 "SMART objectives use observable action verbs such as identify, add or compare, with a condition and standard, so the teacher can check them. \"Understand\" cannot be seen directly; \"Pupils will add two fractions with the same denominator\" can."),
("In the revised Bloom's taxonomy, which task asks pupils to analyse?",
 "Compare the endings of two tales", ["Recite a folk tale aloud", "Retell the story in order", "Write a new folk tale"],
 "Comparing and contrasting break material into parts and examine how they relate, which is analysing. Writing a new tale is creating; retelling is remembering or understanding."),
("A document showing the topics a teacher will cover week by week across a whole term is a:",
 "Scheme of work", ["Lesson note", "Class register", "Report card"],
 "A scheme of work breaks the curriculum into weekly topics for the term. A lesson plan or lesson note details one lesson."),
("In a lesson plan, the part that states what pupils already know before the new topic is the:",
 "Entry behaviour", ["Evaluation", "Lesson summary", "Assignment"],
 "Entry behaviour (previous knowledge) records what learners bring to the lesson so the teacher can build on it. Evaluation checks learning at the end."),
("A pupil answers, \"Plants need water.\" The teacher replies, \"Good. How do you know? What happens to a plant without it?\" These follow-up questions are:",
 "Probing questions", ["Closed questions", "Rhetorical questions", "Leading questions"],
 "Probing questions follow up an answer to push the pupil to explain, justify or go deeper. Closed questions have one short answer, and rhetorical questions expect no answer."),
("A teacher asks a question, gives pupils a minute to think alone, has them discuss with the person beside them, then calls on some pairs to tell the class. This strategy is:",
 "Think-pair-share", ["Brainstorming", "Peer marking", "Choral response"],
 "Think-pair-share gives every pupil thinking time, then a low-risk chance to talk with a partner before sharing with the whole class, so more pupils take part."),
("To practise greetings and polite requests in English, pupils act out buying and selling in a pretend market. This method is:",
 "Role play", ["The lecture method", "A field trip", "Programmed instruction"],
 "In role play, pupils act out real-life situations to practise language and social skills. It is a learner-centred, active method."),
("Which question asks pupils to apply what they learned rather than recall it?",
 "How would you share 24 oranges among 6?", ["What is 4 times 6?", "Who wrote our class rule on the wall?", "What is the capital of Kwara?"],
 "Applying a skill to a new situation, such as sharing oranges, is application. The other questions only ask pupils to recall a fact."),
("After asking a question, a teacher waits about five seconds before calling on anyone. The main benefit is that:",
 "More pupils think of answers", ["The lesson ends sooner", "Fewer pupils answer at all", "Noise stops completely"],
 "Longer wait time gives every pupil time to think, so more pupils answer, and answers are longer and better reasoned."),
("Which comment on a pupil's essay is the most effective feedback?",
 "Clear opening; now add an example", ["Good work, keep it up", "Poor work, see me after class", "7 out of 10"],
 "Effective feedback is specific and tells the learner what to do next. Praise alone or a mark alone does not show how to improve."),
("One teacher handles Primary 1, 2 and 3 in the same room at the same time. This arrangement is:",
 "Multigrade teaching", ["Team teaching", "Micro teaching", "Ability streaming"],
 "Multigrade teaching means one teacher teaching two or more grade levels together, common in small rural schools. Team teaching uses several teachers for one class."),
("Which classroom rule is written in the most effective way?",
 "Raise your hand to speak", ["Don't be rude", "No noise at all, ever", "Behave yourself well"],
 "Good classroom rules are few, clear and positively stated: they tell pupils exactly what to do. \"Don't be rude\" and \"Behave yourself\" are vague, and \"No noise ever\" cannot be kept."),
("While teaching, Mr Okoro walks over and stands near two pupils who are chatting, without stopping the lesson. This technique is:",
 "Proximity control", ["Time-out", "After-school detention", "A public warning"],
 "Proximity control uses the teacher's physical presence to redirect behaviour quietly, without interrupting the lesson or embarrassing pupils."),
("Positive behaviour support mainly works by:",
 "Teaching and praising good conduct", ["Punishing every single small mistake", "Ignoring all behaviour", "Sending pupils home"],
 "Positive behaviour support teaches expected behaviour clearly and reinforces it, preventing problems instead of relying on punishment."),
("A teacher lets pupils show what they learned about the water cycle as a song, a drawing or a short essay. She is differentiating by:",
 "Product", ["Content", "Process", "Assessment date"],
 "Differentiating by product varies how pupils show their learning. Differentiating content varies what is taught; process varies how pupils work on it."),
("The first thing to check when choosing a teaching aid for a lesson is whether it:",
 "Fits the lesson objective", ["Is the newest item in the shop", "Was costly to buy", "Is brightly coloured"],
 "An instructional resource must be relevant to the objective first; then consider accessibility, cost and safety."),
("Which of these is a teacher-centred method?",
 "The lecture method", ["Group project work", "Role play", "Field trips"],
 "In the lecture method the teacher talks and pupils mainly listen. Projects, role play and field trips put learners at the centre."),
("Which school record shows each pupil's daily presence in class?",
 "The attendance register", ["The school's daily log book", "The scheme of work", "The visitors' book"],
 "The class attendance register records daily attendance. The log book records important events in the life of the school."),
("A teacher sets up a reading corner, a counting table and a drawing table, and pupils rotate through them. She is differentiating by:",
 "Process", ["Product", "Content", "Grading"],
 "Learning centres vary how pupils work on the material, which is differentiation by process."),
("Two pupils are arguing over a seat. The best first step for the teacher is to:",
 "Hear each side calmly", ["Punish both of them at once", "Ignore them both", "Shout to stop them"],
 "Conflict resolution starts by calming things and letting each pupil explain, so the teacher can guide them to a fair solution."),
]
